<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julius Civilis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/69">69</a>–<a href="/years/70">70</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Gaius Julius Civilis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Julius Civilis was a Batavian noble and Roman auxiliary commander who led the Batavian revolt of 69 to 70 CE during the civil wars of the Year of the Four Emperors. Joined by the Cananefates, Frisii and Treveri, the rebels destroyed several Roman forts on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. The Roman general Petillius Cerialis ended the revolt in 70 CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-civilis.jpg" alt="Julius Civilis" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brigade Piron · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tervuren_-_%22Claudius_Civilis%22_by_Lodewijk_Van_Geel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gaius Julius Civilis was born in 25 CE, and is sometimes wrongly called Claudius Civilis. His nomen shows that he, or one of his male ancestors, was made a Roman citizen by Augustus or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a>, which suggests that his tribe became a Roman vassal then. He was twice imprisoned on a charge of rebellion and narrowly escaped execution. The Batavians had given valuable service under the early emperors and had been well treated to attach them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. They paid no tribute but had to supply many men for the army, and the burden of conscription and the oppression of provincial governors were important incentives to revolt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>During the disturbances that followed the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>, Civilis took up arms, pretending to side with Vespasian, and induced the people of his native country to rebel. Several neighbouring Germanic tribes soon joined the Batavians. The Roman garrisons near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> were driven out and twenty-four ships captured. Two legions under Mummius Lupercus were defeated at Castra Vetera, near modern Xanten, and surrounded. Eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a> of Batavian veterans joined their countrymen, and the troops Vespasian sent to relieve Vetera threw in their lot with them.</p><p>The rising spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. In 70 CE Hordeonius Flaccus was murdered by his troops, and two commanders of Gallic auxiliaries, Julius Classicus and Julius Tutor, induced the Roman forces to revolt and join Civilis. Gaul practically declared itself independent, and the foundation of a new kingdom of Gaul was contemplated. The prophetess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veleda">Veleda</a> predicted that Civilis would succeed and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> would fall. But disputes among the tribes made cooperation impossible.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Vespasian, having ended the civil war, called on Civilis to lay down his arms and, when he refused, decided to suppress the revolt by force. The arrival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-petillius-cerialis">Quintus Petillius Cerialis</a> with a strong force awed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> and the mutinous troops into submission. Civilis was defeated at Augusta Treverorum, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, and at Castra Vetera, and withdrew to the island of the Batavians. He finally came to an agreement with Cerialis under which his countrymen obtained certain advantages and resumed friendly relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. From then on he disappears from history, and nothing is recorded of his death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gaius Julius Civilis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 69 CE <b>Julius Civilis</b> led a revolt joined by the Cananefates, Frisii and Treveri, which was crushed in 70 CE.</blockquote><a href="/state/batavi">Batavi</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Brinno they attacked Roman forts on the coast at the start of the revolt of <b>Julius Civilis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/cananefates">Cananefates</a></li></ul></section>
